section('opening')
p('**Research judgment as of 6 October 2026.** Pharvaris has persuasive randomized evidence that deucrictibant treats and prevents hereditary angioedema attacks. That supports further investment work, but does not yet establish an attractive stock. Both pivotal efficacy readouts are complete. The most valuable remaining questions concern paid access, sustained use, incremental benefit over current alternatives, and the interaction between prevention and acute consumption. This memo identifies the evidence that can enter a model and the evidence still needed to demonstrate a valuation disagreement; it does not produce a DCF or price target.')
p('The strongest prospective long thesis is a smaller, reachable population generating more paid months because control and tolerability make patients stay. The strongest counterthesis is that existing oral treatments, increasingly infrequent injections, payer restrictions, and a potentially imminent one-time treatment constrain share and net economics. Public evidence establishes these mechanisms more confidently than their magnitude. A claim that either is already mispriced would exceed the evidence.')
