section('quant')
h1('Quantitative architecture for a future SOTP')
p('A sum-of-the-parts valuation, or SOTP, separates indication cash flows while preserving shared costs and risks. Build the operating model first. Every input should carry a definition, unit, date, population and status: observed, derived, proxy, assumed or unknown. Arithmetic checks establish that formulas are correct; calibration asks whether inputs match observed behavior; independent validation tests the model on different data. None substitutes for another.')
h2('Diagnosis and treatment cohorts')
eq('U[t+1] = U[t] + incident symptomatic cases − new diagnoses − exits\nnew diagnoses[t] = min(U[t] × diagnosis hazard[t], pathway capacity[t])\nD[t+1] = D[t] + new diagnoses[t] + net migration − deaths or corrections')
p('U is the undiagnosed symptomatic population; D is the diagnosed stock, each measured in unique people. The diagnosis hazard is the probability of diagnosis in a period among those still undiagnosed. Capacity includes referral, testing and specialist evaluation. Inherited susceptibility is not the same as symptomatic expression or an incident diagnosed case. Family screening discovers existing prevalent cases and must be deduplicated against routine referrals. Changes in definitions and database coverage should be modeled separately from biological incidence. Diagnosis rates may fall after easier cases are found.')
eq('New paid starts = eligible decision opportunities\n  × P(offered | eligible) × P(accept | offered)\n  × P(covered | accepted) × P(dispensed | covered)')
p('Eligibility is the intersection of label, clinical appropriateness and reachable care, not the product of unrelated marginal percentages. Apply conditional probabilities to avoid counting the same access loss twice. Decision opportunities include new diagnoses, existing patients choosing therapy and switches. A switch moves a person between treatment states; it does not add to disease prevalence. Separate first paid starts from free bridge starts and transfers between products.')
eq('Active[c,t] = starts[c] × S(c,t)\nPaid patient-months[t] = Σc Active[c,t] × months exposed[c,t] × paid fraction[c,t]\nProphylaxis net revenue[t] = paid patient-months[t] × net revenue per paid month[t]')
p('S(c,t) is the probability that start cohort c remains on treatment at time t. A survival curve is more informative than one average annual retention rate because early intolerance and later stopping differ. The paid fraction includes coverage and free-drug status conditional on remaining treated. If the empirical survival curve already measures paid persistence, do not apply that attrition again. Adherence means taking doses as directed while on therapy; it may change attacks even when the bottle was paid for. Missed fills already reflected in paid units must not receive a second revenue haircut.')
h2('Acute demand and inventory')
eq('Consumed PHVS doses[t] = Σg patient-months[g,t] × λ[g,t] × f[g,t] × q[g,t] × d[g,t]\nPaid dispensed doses = consumption + change in home stock + replacements − free doses\nAcute net revenue = paid doses × net revenue per dose')
p('Segment g identifies prevention state, subtype, age and access. λ is residual attacks per patient-month, f the fraction treated, q PHVS share of treated attacks, and d doses per PHVS-treated attack. Track redosing and use of another rescue product separately. Replacement covers expiration or loss. Manufacturer revenue also depends on changes in distributor inventory, which is distinct from patient-held stock. Match price to dose, tablet or pack consistently: two tablets may constitute one treatment dose.')
p('Treatment changes must update the same patients’ acute demand. Prevention success lowers λ; more convenient rescue may raise f; faster relief may lower d; better experience may raise q. The direction of total acute revenue is therefore an empirical question. Better acute treatment might delay prevention initiation for some patients, but that belongs in a tested transition scenario, not an automatic benefit to both franchises.')
h2('Worked example 1  What attack freedom does and does not establish')
p('**Observed PHVS inputs; derived approximation.** In CHAPTER-3, 25/55 treated participants versus 1/30 placebo participants had no attacks over 24 weeks: 45.5% versus 3.3%. The unadjusted risk difference is 42.1 percentage points. Wilson 95% intervals for the two proportions are approximately 33.0–58.5% and 0.6–16.7%; a Newcombe interval for the difference is approximately 23.9–55.4 points. These score-based intervals behave better than a simple symmetric normal interval when one group has only one responder.')
p('This supports a large advantage over placebo, with uncertainty around the proportion of future patients achieving complete control. It does not show that at least 60% will be attack-free, that stable patients will switch, or that PHVS beats an active comparator. The calculation uses published binary counts and does not reproduce the baseline-adjusted Poisson primary analysis, the trial’s multiplicity procedure, or its protocol-defined missing-data handling. It enters a control-expectation sensitivity, not an automatic market-share conversion.')
p('RAPIDe-3’s missing paired observations pose a different problem: aggregate counts cannot reconstruct an all-randomized time-to-relief distribution. A useful sensitivity would compare first-period randomized treatment, all treated attacks with within-person correlation, and assumptions that unobserved outcomes differ progressively from observed outcomes. The tipping point is the assumption at which the conclusion changes. Assigning synthetic relief times or treating every missing attack as clinical failure would manufacture precision.')
h2('Worked example 2  More paid treatment without more diagnoses')
p('**Hypothetical architecture, not PHVS estimates.** Begin with 100 newly confirmed diagnoses. Assume 75% reach specialist care, 80% are label-eligible and clinically suitable, 50% are offered the product, 80% accept, 65% obtain initial coverage and 95% dispense. Expected paid starts are 100 × .75 × .80 × .50 × .80 × .65 × .95 = 14.82. If each starter generates nine expected paid months during the twelve months after starting, the cohort produces 11.115 paid patient-years.')
p('Raise initial coverage to 80% and expected paid months to 10.5, leaving diagnoses and all other inputs unchanged. Starts become 18.24 and paid patient-years become 15.96, a 43.6% increase. These are twelve-month post-start windows, not a calendar-year forecast; a calendar model must place each start on its actual date. The improvement may require rebates or commercial spending, so revenue growth does not imply an equal increase in operating value.')
h2('Worked example 3  Prevention grows while acute units decline')
p('**Hypothetical fixed cohort of 1,000 patients; calendar-month units.** Initially 400 use prevention at 0.25 attacks per month and 600 use acute treatment alone at 2 attacks per month. Annual attacks total 15,600. At 60% of attacks treated, 30% PHVS acute share and 1.1 doses per PHVS-treated attack, annual PHVS consumption is 3,088.8 doses.')
p('In a better-prevention scenario, 700 patients use prevention at 0.15 attacks per month and 300 remain acute-only at 2 per month. Total attacks fall to 8,460. Even if 90% of attacks are treated, unchanged share and dosing yield 2,512.62 PHVS doses, down 18.7%, while prevention patient-years rise from 400 to 700. PHVS acute share would need to reach approximately 36.9%, rather than 30%, to preserve initial consumption. At 45% share it would reach 3,768.93 doses.')
p('This scenario holds inventory, free drug and price constant to isolate utilization. It neither predicts those rates nor rules out acute growth. Clinical four-week attack rates are not calendar-month rates: use a consistent conversion before inserting them into annual demand. The commercial model must jointly determine prevention uptake, residual attacks, treatment propensity and product share.')
h2('Probabilities and economic dependencies')
eq('P(both approvals) = P(common molecule gate passes)\n  × P(IR and XR approval | common gate passes)\nExpected operating value = Σs p[s] × PV(cash flows along scenario s), with Σs p[s] = 1')
p('The common gate can include molecule-level safety. Conditional risks include formulation performance, manufacturing, filing completeness and indication-specific label decisions. Do not multiply two independent generic approval rates. Clinical endpoint success has already been observed for RAPIDe-3 and CHAPTER-3; approval, useful label and commercial performance conditional on approval remain separate questions. A Bayesian update would need a relevant post-pivotal reference class and justified likelihoods. This memo does not invent numerical likelihood ratios or count multiple publications of one trial as independent evidence.')
p('Coherent scenarios should include both approvals, IR only, XR only, delayed or restricted approvals, and common failure. Within approved scenarios, vary access, persistence, competition and pricing together. A broad-access win might involve lower net price; gene-editing uptake might reduce both chronic treatment and rescue consumption. Failed paths still incur development and launch preparation costs until the failure date. Explicit failure probabilities and a discount rate address different concepts; multiple arbitrary safety haircuts would count the same risk repeatedly.')
p('For each geography and indication, deduct manufacturing and distribution costs, royalties, remaining development, launch costs, taxes and working capital from net sales. Deduct the shared selling organization and corporate costs once. Gross-to-net deductions convert list prices to realized prices through rebates and other adjustments; do not deduct them again from a net-price input. Model patents and regulatory exclusivity by territory and claim, with erosion after loss of protection. Do not assume a proportional disappearance of the cost base when price falls.')
table(['Evidence','Parameter and quantitative treatment','Admission and value implication'],[
['Faster dependable relief','Change treated fraction, acute share and redose probability separately.','Sensitivity until causal behavior observed; stronger clinical effect is not equal percentage share gain.'],
['Reliable prevention','Change switching and paid survival; lower residual acute attack rate simultaneously.','Efficacy observed; commercial conversion unknown. Benefit to XR can offset IR units.'],
['Narrow responsive cohort','Restrict eligibility, potentially raise cohort uptake and persistence.','No validated PHVS subgroup uplift yet; smaller market may have higher yield.'],
['Safety or interaction limitation','Change approval/label, monitoring cost, prescribing and stopping along one causal path.','Use joint scenarios; avoid repeated independent penalties for the same event.'],
['Access and persistence','Conditional paid conversion × start timing × survival × realized price.','Mechanism supported; PHVS coefficients unknown. Multiplicative effect can be material.'],
['Diagnosis growth','Bounded additions from undiagnosed stock with subtype-specific conversion.','Sensitivity; sales growth cannot calibrate diagnosis without decomposition.']
],[1.5,2.83,2.73])
h2('Cash funding and the equity denominator')
p('June 2026 financial statements report 70,204,506 shares outstanding, €318 million cash, 4,455,397 options and 1,545,152 restricted share units. The 6.0 million awards are not all currently dilutive; vesting, exercise prices and valuation date matter. Loss-year diluted earnings per share excludes anti-dilutive awards and is therefore not a reliable valuation share count. The May financing is already included in June cash and shares; do not add its proceeds again. [F5]')
p('Q2 research and development expense was €35.0 million and general and administrative expense €15.8 million. Management’s stated runway into 2028 is a forecast, not proof that every launch scenario is funded. June commitments of about €118 million principally relate to research and development; subtracting them from equity after forecasting the same expense would double count them. Roll cash forward using actual operating, capital and financing cash flows, not net loss alone. [F1] [F5]')
eq('Equity value = operating value + valuation-date cash − debt\n  − obligations not already included in operating cash flows\nPer-share value = scenario-consistent equity value / scenario-consistent shares')
p('Model future funding with its date, fees, issue price and resulting shares. Do not both subtract an entire financing requirement and omit the cash received while also diluting shares. Stock compensation is an economic cost: if added back to cash flow, model future dilution consistently. Convert euro financials and dollar share prices at an explicitly dated exchange rate.')

section('expectations')
h1('An insight is not yet a demonstrated market disagreement')
p('The 5 October 2026 closing price was $30.49 in two public quote/forecast services. Multiplying by the June share count gives an indicative basic equity value of about $2.14 billion. This deliberately mixes a current price with the latest verified balance-sheet share count and is neither current fully diluted equity value nor enterprise value. One quote feed implied a materially older share count; the filing is the stronger denominator. A valuation requires subsequent issuance, cash burn, awards, debt and currency reconciliation. [F5] [F6] [F7]')
p('The same forecast aggregators show broadly favorable analyst sentiment but differ in analyst counts. They do not provide a verified, dated product-level consensus for IR or XR sales, margins, access or persistence. It is therefore inappropriate to assert that the market assumes a particular peak penetration or has ignored cannibalization. Company claims about oral convenience document promotion, not investor beliefs.')
p('A reverse valuation should solve for multiple combinations consistent with enterprise value. Even annual revenue of $300 million could hypothetically represent 3,000 paid patient-years at $100,000 net each or 1,500 at $200,000; costs and risk would further distinguish them. Likewise, a higher IR contribution and lower XR contribution can produce the same total. A stock price is one observation and cannot uniquely identify many operating assumptions.')
p('Separate three outcomes: a catalyst reaction depends on surprise versus expectations; operating value depends on future cash flows; shareholder return also depends on entry valuation, financing and time. Two successful studies can coexist with disappointing returns if the share price already demands favorable access, expensive launch infrastructure delays cash generation, or new competitors change treatment choice. The research supports an investable set of questions, not a verified “unpriced” conclusion.')

